-- title:
--   Vanishing of the independence element and cyclic subgroup membership
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field, $W$ a Weierstrass curve over $F$ (no condition on its discriminant is imposed), and $p$ a prime with $p \neq 2$. Let $(x_0,y_0)$ and $(x,y)$ be pairs of elements of $F$ that are nonsingular points of the associated affine curve `W.toAffine`, giving affine points $P_0 = (x_0,y_0)$ and $P = (x,y)$ of the group of points, and assume $p \cdot P_0 = 0$. Then the independence element
--   $$\mathrm{indepElt}(W,p,x_0,x) \;=\; \prod_{a=1}^{\lfloor (p-1)/2\rfloor}\bigl(x\,\Psi^2_a(x_0) - \Phi_a(x_0)\bigr),$$
--   formed from the division polynomials $\Psi^2_a$ and $\Phi_a$ of $W$ (so that $x([a]Q) = \Phi_a(x(Q))/\Psi^2_a(x(Q))$), vanishes if and only if $P$ lies in the subgroup `AddSubgroup.zmultiples` of integer multiples of $P_0$. Note that the product ranges over the natural-number subtraction $p-1$ halved, i.e. over $1 \le a \le (p-1)/2$, and that the equivalence is with membership in the full cyclic group generated by $P_0$, not merely with equality of $x$-coordinates for some $a$ in that range.
--
--   This is the field-theoretic dictionary between the ring-theoretic independence condition used in the definition of Katz level-$p$ structures — invertibility, respectively vanishing, of the independence element — and the geometric condition that a point lie in the cyclic subgroup generated by a $p$-torsion point. It is used throughout the treatment of full level structures on modular curves, for instance in the determinant and relabelling computations for the curves of level $\Gamma_0(p^n)$ and in the construction of rigidified Tate data.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_indepElt_eq_zero_iff_mem_zmultiples.lean

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ModularCurve_KatzLevelP

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
